  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a comprehensive overview of what opioids are derived from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\n### \ud83c\udf3a The Opium Poppy \u2014 The Original Source\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. In its raw state, opium is a gummy latex extracted from *Papaver somniferum*.\n\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about **3,000 BCE**, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and for producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks.\n\n---\n\n### The Three Categories of Opioids\n\nOpioids are generally broken down into three types based on their origin:\n\n#### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nNatural opioids are substances derived **directly from the opium poppy**, typically from the resin of the plant's seed pod. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**.\n\nSome opioid drugs are made from naturally occurring plant compounds (**alkaloids**) that come from a specific type of poppy plant called an opium poppy.\n\nExamples include: **codeine, heroin, morphine, and opium**.\n\n#### 2. \u2697\ufe0f Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that pharmaceutical manufacturers (or drug traffickers) use to make semi-synthetic opioids.\n\nA few opioid molecules \u2014 **hydrocodone** (e.g., Vicodin), **hydromorphone** (e.g., Dilaudid), **oxycodone** (e.g., OxyContin, Percocet) \u2014 may be partially synthesized from chemical components of opium.\n\n#### 3. \ud83e\uddea Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are **entirely man-made**. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are not made from products of the opium poppy. Instead, they are made from other **precursor chemicals** that do not depend on the opium poppy.\n\nSynthetic opioid compounds are made to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors. Examples include fentanyl and methadone.\n\n---\n\n### How Do They Work?\nOpioids are a class of drugs that derive from, or mimic, natural substances found in the opium poppy plant. They work in the **brain** to produce a variety of effects, including pain relief.\n\nExperiments with opiates led to the development of synthetic compounds that have similar effects on the body. To distinguish them from natural opiates, these synthetic compounds were called **opioids** (meaning \"opiate-like\").",
